  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals a few shortwave ripples in \nzonal flow aloft forcing mid-level isentropic ascent. So far the \nbest ascent has been focused across north and central WI with only \nscattered showers lifting into the western half of the UP. Given the \ncurrent trajectory of precip on radar and model precip struggling to \nlift much rain into the northern tier of the UP, thinking the bulk \nof rainfall will be found in the southern half. CAM ensembles are \nstill meh about healthy widespread rain, only showing a 10-30% \nchance for amounts greater than a quarter inch anywhere north of \nMenominee County. As showers skirt east tonight, increased surface \nmoisture (dewpoints in the 60s) combined with light winds and area \nrainfall will help patchy dense fog develop through day break. Areas \nof dense fog will be more common across the interior, especially \nareas that saw more rainfall.\n\nWeak height rises on Sunday should hold dry conditions for the UP \ntomorrow though cannot rule out some spotty precip redeveloping \nacross the south half (10-30% chance). The next best shot at more \nmeaningful rainfall returns Monday with a warm front lifting into \nthe Great Lakes. Surging moisture brings PWATs between 1.5-2\" (~99th \npercentile of NAEFS climo for this time of year up here) and MUCAPE \nvalues >1500 j/kg. Should bulk shear 35-45 kts be present, a \nmarginal severe weather threat may shape up as convection lifts \nahead of this boundary. Ensembles aren't sold on heavy rain quite \nyet (25-50% chance for amounts >0.25\"), but I suspect CAMs will pick \nup on a heavy rain threat once we get closer to the event. The tail \nend of the incoming 12z HREF shows pockets of 1-1.5\" of rain per 6hr \nLPMM. This is a welcomed sight as drought concerns grow headed into \nthe typical \"second fire season\" up here.\n\nSome additional rain showers and thunderstorms could be seen behind \nthe warm front Monday evening into Tuesday. However, rain chances \ndiminish from the mid-week until near the end of the forecast period \nas high pressure ridging rebuilds over the central CONUS. Thus, rain \nchances will be relegated to mesoscale forcing mechanisms (for \nexample, the lake breeze) due to the lack of synoptic-scale forcing. \nBecause warm air from the Gulf is continuing to be pumped over the \nregion behind the warm front, expect the above normal temperatures \nand humid conditions seen this weekend through early next week to \ncontinue through the rest of next week.",
